# The worker, run from source: `cp deploy/worker.env.example deploy/worker.env`, then
# `pnpm --filter @alloy-works/worker dev`, which reads that copy. The copy is git-ignored; this
# example is not, and the containers in compose.yaml do not read either.
#
# The passwords and the key are local
# development defaults for throwaway containers bound to 127.0.0.1 - never credentials for anything
# deployed.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://aw_worker:aw_worker_dev@127.0.0.1:5432/alloy_dev
OBJECT_STORE_ENDPOINT=http://127.0.0.1:8333
OBJECT_STORE_BUCKET=alloy-dev
SECRET_OBJECT_STORE_KEY=ZGV2ZWxvcG1lbnQtb25seS1vYmplY3Qta2V5LTAwMDE=
LOG_LEVEL=info
# veraPDF, which checks every publication's PDF: its launcher, where one is installed, with Java 11 or
# later. The worker image holds it at /opt/verapdf/verapdf; without one, checks are given up and a
# publication stays unchecked, the sweep queues its check again, and after three give-ups it is left
# and its page says it could not be checked.
# VERAPDF_COMMAND=/opt/verapdf/verapdf
# veraPDF's JVM options. The worker gives the JVM -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=50, half the memory it is
# given, where these name no heap limit (-Xmx, -XX:MaxRAMPercentage, -XX:MaxRAM, -XX:MaxHeapSize), and
# veraPDF none of the rest of the worker's environment.
# JAVA_OPTS=-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=50
# How long a claimed job is held before another worker may take it: two minutes by default. veraPDF
# is given a third of it to start and a third to check a PDF, so a check never outlives its lease.
# LEASE_MS=120000
